      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat: add Twilio RCS channel [AI]",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nOpenClaw's official Twilio SMS channel can receive RCS-flavored addresses, but it does not provide a first-class RCS channel surface for rich content, delivery/read receipts, RCS sender configuration, or deterministic SMS/RCS webhook ownership.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis adds a bundled `extensions/rcs` channel shaped after the official SMS extension while keeping provider-specific behavior inside the Twilio adapter. It includes:\n\n- canonical `rcs:+E164` addressing and SMS/RCS route-collision protection\n- rich text/media/content-card sends\n- persistent delivery/read receipts in shared SQLite state\n- persistent replay protection\n- Twilio signature and AccountSid validation\n- generated plugin/config/docs surfaces and SecretRef coverage\n- explicit HTTP registration conflict policy so SMS and RCS cannot silently claim the same route\n\nThe branch remains proposed as a bundled official channel. The maintainer scope decision and exact-head dependency approval are intentionally left to maintainers; this PR does not externalize the implementation.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOperators with an approved Twilio RCS sender can configure RCS as a normal OpenClaw channel, receive inbound RCS messages, send rich responses, and observe durable delivery/read status without relying on an SMS-only compatibility path.\n\nExisting SMS deployments are unchanged unless they configure RCS on a colliding webhook route, in which case startup fails clearly instead of double-registering or silently dispatching to the wrong channel.\n\n## Evidence\n\nCurrent contributor head: `9f15df87905` on upstream `main` `e5b2ca3c6bd`.\n\n- Focused current-branch validation: 13 test files, 109 tests passed across the full RCS suite plus SMS gateway coexistence and core HTTP-registry coverage.\n- Positive coverage includes account/config resolution, canonical addresses, inbound/outbound content, durable receipts, `EventType=READ`, replay protection, guarded Twilio API handling, and shared-route ownership.\n- Negative coverage includes invalid signatures/accounts, replayed callbacks, route collisions, malformed provider responses, unsafe content, and conflicting SMS/RCS ownership.\n- `node scripts/generate-plugin-inventory-doc.mjs --check` passed.\n- `node scripts/generate-docs-map.mjs --check` passed.\n- bundled channel config metadata and config-doc baseline checks passed through their direct TypeScript generators.\n- `git diff --check upstream/main...HEAD` passed.\n- The pre-PR plan gate passed and identifies the expected maintainer-only dependency approval for `extensions/rcs/package.json` / `pnpm-lock.yaml`.\n\n### Real behavior proof\n\nThe code paths are exercised through the real extension handlers/adapters with deterministic provider doubles. Earlier live Twilio RCS work informed the transport contract, but this exact head has not been deployed against an approved RCS sender/device. The current environment reports RCS unconfigured, and no Gateway restart, carrier activation, paid send, or production topology change was performed for this refresh.\n\nRemaining non-code gates are therefore explicit: maintainer scope acceptance, dependency approval on the exact pushed head, and approved-sender/device proof if maintainers require it.\n\nRelated: #88476, #102373, #104862, #105710.\n\nAI-assisted; reviewed and validated before submission.\n",
